# Docker build context
#
# The build context is the set of files Docker is allowed to access while building
# an image. Docker Compose uses the repository root as the build context, so files
# in this directory may otherwise be scanned, hashed, or transferred to the builder
# even when a Dockerfile never copies them into the image.
#
# Excluding unnecessary files keeps the build context smaller. This reduces
# filesystem work and data transfer and can make builds faster, especially when
# directories contain many files or large amounts of runtime data.
#
# These exclusions do not delete or modify local files, and they do not affect
# directories mounted into running containers by Docker Compose.
#
# The Dockerfiles in this project already COPY only the configuration files they
# need. Therefore, these rules mainly reduce build-context overhead and prevent
# irrelevant files from becoming build inputs; they do not significantly reduce
# the size of the resulting images.
#
# .dockerignore is evaluated by Docker independently of .gitignore.

# Application files and runtime data are provided to containers through Compose
# mounts and are not required while building the images.
www/
dbdata/
iedata/
log/

# Git history and metadata are irrelevant to the image build.
.git/

# Python bytecode and cache directories are generated locally and are not needed
# by the builder.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]